      "Ancient populations of working dogs → encounters and selections in the Mediterranean and the Balkans → types known in the Greek and Epirote world → a plurality of strong dogs in the Roman world → local functional populations in Italy → working traditions in which, many centuries later, we recognize the Cane Corso.",
      "This is not a genealogy.",
      "It is a **hypothesis of functional and cultural continuity**, within which biological continuities may also have existed that we are not able to reconstruct in full today.",
      "It is possible that Rome helped move dogs and traits among different territories. It is possible that animals arriving in the provinces mixed with local populations. It is possible that, once the military function ended or the political context changed, some qualities were reused in guarding, hunting, protecting property, or managing animals.",
      "But the word **possible** must remain visible until the documentation allows us to say more.",
      "Stefano",
      "At first I almost wanted to find a road on the map.",
      "Mesopotamia. Epirus. Rome. Southern Italy. Cane Corso.",
      "Then I understood that Dott. Flavio Bruno’s lesson was more difficult.",
