      "They are three different things.",
      "Art Is Not a Pedigree",
      "The Renaissance gives us extraordinary images. Powerful dogs appear in scenes of hunting, battle, property, and aristocratic life. But a painting, however realistic, does not automatically give us a pedigree.",
      "Every image gains value when it is read through authorship, dating, provenance, subject, and context. Morphological similarities remain observations, not genealogical proof.",
      "This caution does not diminish the value of images. It increases it.",
      "If we stop asking “is it a Cane Corso or not?”, we can ask something more interesting:",
      "What was that dog doing?",
      "Who was it with?",
      "In what environment?",
      "What kind of body did that function require?",
      "This is where Dott. Flavio Bruno always brings me back.",
      "Not to form in isolation.",
      "To the relationship between form and necessity.",
      "Function shapes type.",
      "The Renaissance brings us closer to the name, but it does not close the question. To understand why the type continues to exist, we must follow the dog out of the images and into the work of the Early Modern Age."
